Facing the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit, declining the pawn with 2...Nf6 allowed White to seize space with 3.e5. After your knight retreated, you tried activating your bishop with 5...Bb4, setting up a pin on the c3 knight, but exchanging it off with 6...Bxc3+ left you without the dark-squared bishop to help defend your kingside.","line":"2.e4 Nf6 3.e5 Nfd7 4.Nc3 e6"},{"text":"Castling directly into White's primed attack with 7...O-O proved extremely dangerous against the looming light-squared bishop on d3. White missed the cleanest shot with 8.Bxh7+, instead opting for 8.Ng5, which you met well with 8...h6.","line":"7...O-O 8.Ng5 h6 9.Qh5"},{"text":"Under heavy pressure from White's queen and minor pieces, 9...f5 opened up fatal weaknesses. White capitalized immediately with 10.Nxe6, capturing a pawn and tearing into your position, which prompted an early resignation.","line":"9...f5 10.Nxe6"}],"turning_point":"The decisive moment was 7...O-O, where castling into White's active bishop on d3 gave White a crushing kingside attack.","focus":"Take free central pawns in gambit openings rather than allowing your pieces to get kicked backwards, as happened with 2...Nf6.","model":"google/gemini-3.7-flash","v":2,"cost":0.002514,"at":1793768548483}}
